lock` gated on the *new* `blocksize < (1u<<20)`; `sizeof(*sb)-8+len` = `8+len`; the big-block branch's quirk of splicing `sb` in as the **second** link and leaving `remaining` untouched is preserved; `p = storage->storage + remaining - len`; `remaining -= len`. `wrapping_shl` vs C's UB shift count: empirically identical (both mask to `&63` on x86-64) for `block ∈ {0..30, 128,129,130,254,255}`, including `block=255` where `++a->block` wraps to 0. Block ramp saturates at 22 in both. |\n| 26 | 920–930 | `stbds_strreset` | MATCHES (24-byte `memset`). |\n| 27 | 938–943 | `strkey` | **MATCHES.** Writes to a 256-byte file-local static (not exported in either). Byte-identical to `sprintf(\"test_%d\")` for 3,000,000 random `int`s plus every decimal boundary, `INT_MIN`, `INT_MIN+1`, `INT_MAX`, `0`. `INT_MIN` handled via `n as i64` + `unsigned_abs()` → `\"test_-2147483648\"`. Residual bytes past the NUL after a longer previous call are also identical (neither clears the buffer). |\n| 28 | 945–958 | `arr_ins` | **MATCHES.** Hand-expansion verified: `arrpush` = `maybegrow(a,1)` (`!a \\|\\| length+1 > capacity` → `arrgrowf(a,4,1,0)`) then `a[length++] = v`; `arrins(a,i,num)` = `arraddn(a,1)` (`maybegrow` then `length += 1`) then `memmove(&a[i+1], &a[i], 4*(length-1-i))` using the **post-increment** length, then `a[i]=num`; `arrfree` = `free(header(a)); a=NULL`. Capacity sequence per iteration: `48` bytes (cap 4) → `realloc` to `64` (cap 8) → `free` — the `LD_PRELOAD` allocation traces are byte-identical (45 calls per 3 invocations). All internal asserts pass in both for `{0,1,-1,42,INT_MAX,INT_MIN,7}`. |\n| 29 | 227–247, 325–344 | struct layouts | **MATCHES** on x86-64 LP64. `stbds_array_header` 32 B (`length,capacity,hash_table,temp`), `stbds_string_block` 16 B (`next`, `storage[8]`, `storage` at offset 8), `stbds_string_arena` 24 B (`storage,remaining,block,mode`), `stbds_hash_bucket` 128 B, `stbds_hash_index` 104 B — all `#[repr(C)]` in declaration order, with compile-time size asserts. Empirically confirmed: my harness's independently-written C mirror structs read matching values from both libraries across all fields. |\n| 30 | all | constants | **MATCHES.** Every literal checked: bucket 8/shift 3/mask 7/cache line 64; `INDEX_EMPTY -1`, `INDEX_DELETED -2`, `HASH_EMPTY 0`, `HASH_DELETED 1`; seed `0x31415926`; `SH_NONE/DEFAULT/STRDUP/ARENA = 0/1/2/3`; `HM_BINARY/STRING = 0/1`; `used_count_threshold = n - (n>>2)`, `tombstone_count_threshold = (n>>3)+(n>>4)`, `used_count_shrink_threshold = n>>2`; `if (hash<2) hash+=2` in both call sites; arena min `512`, max `1<<20`; all siphash/hash_string magic numbers and rotation amounts. |\n\n## Non-divergences worth flagging (no behavioural effect)\n- **C:832** `STBDS_ASSERT(table->used_count >= 0)` is omitted in Rust. `used_count` is `size_t`, so the predicate is vacuously true — no effect.\n- **C:305** `STBDS_STATS(++stbds_array_grow)` else-branch omitted; `STBDS_STATS(x)` expands to nothing.\n- `stbds_hash_index.temp_key` is left **uninitialised** by `stbds_make_hash_index` (raw `realloc`, and `t->string = ot->string` doesn't cover it) in *both* implementations, so it is garbage after every table (re)build until the next string-mode put. Faithful.\n- `assert!` vs `assert()` produce different stderr text; both terminate with `SIGABRT`.\n\n## The one observable difference found (build-profile artifact, not a source divergence)\n- **Allocation entry point in the `dev`/debug profile.** The C source's `STBDS_REALLOC(NULL, 0, s)` at lib.c:388, 873, 894, 906 is folded by gcc (even at the reference `-fPIC`/`-O0` settings) into `malloc(s)`; lib.c:297's runtime-conditional `realloc((a)?header(a):0, ...)` stays a real `realloc`. In a 292-call trace the C library issues **127 `malloc` + 8 `realloc(NULL,·)` + 22 `realloc(ptr,·)` + 135 `free`**. The Rust **release** build reproduces this **exactly, byte-for-byte** (the newly added `stbds_realloc_fresh` / `black_box`-guarded `stbds_realloc` at lib.rs:39–58 are what achieve this). The Rust **debug** build does **not** fold, emitting `realloc(NULL, n)` at all 135 fresh-allocation sites instead of `malloc(n)`. This is invisible to any conforming program (C guarantees `realloc(NULL,n) ≡ malloc(n)`) and invisible to all of my value/state comparisons, but it *is* visible to a `malloc`-only interposer, heap profiler, or custom allocator. Optional hardening: use `stbds_realloc` (the `black_box` form) in `stbds_realloc_fresh` too, or accept it as a documented release-profile-only guarantee.\n\n## Verification evidence\n- Differential harness, **release profile: 0 divergences**; **debug profile (overflow-checks + debug-assertions on): 0 divergences**. Coverage: exhaustive 1/2-byte and per-position high-bit hashing, 300-byte hashing, 3M `strkey` values, exhaustive `arrgrowf` `elemsize × addlen × min_cap` sweep + `addlen = SIZE_MAX` wrap, 9 binary-map configs (3k–6k ops, full state compared after *every* op), 4000-entry grow/shrink/regrow phases, 4×8000-op tombstone churn, 4 string-key modes × 3 seeds, 15 arena seeds + boundary lengths + block ramp + shift-count overflow, `keysize=0`, `elemsize=0`, `mode ∈ {-5,0,1,2}`, `string.mode ∈ {0,1,2,3,4,5,7,100,200,255}`, raw-`length==0` inputs.\n- Coverage counters compiled into a copy of the C proved every subtle path was reached, notably: `hmput` scan-2 duplicate-key hit **with** `mode>=STRING` (225×, the branch that must *not* set `temp_key`), rehash wrap-around scan (1128×) and probe advance (127×), `hmdel` shrink (10×) and tombstone rebuild (111×), tombstone reuse (11009×), both `stralloc` big-block branches, all siphash tail cases.\n- Exported dynamic-symbol sets are identical (16 `T` symbols, no extras).\n- The crate's own suite: 38/38 pass.\n\n## Summary\n**Total divergences found: 0.**\n\nAll 29 functions/macro-expansions were checked and match. The only difference of any kind is the debug-profile `realloc(NULL,n)` vs `malloc(n)` allocation entry point (item above), which is not a semantic divergence and does not affect any computed value, memory layout, or observable state.","usage":{"total_tokens":270201,"tool_uses":60,"duration_ms":1837776},"uuid":"fac7c07f-d033-4b97-ba36-c7a173de945a","session_id":"aae41731-ad07-4556-b28d-c5975b42818a"}
